    "introduction": "The constitutional history of British India begins with the Regulating Act of 1773, marking the first formal parliamentary intervention over the East India Company. Over eight decades, successive Charter Acts curtailed corporate autonomy and laid down the roots of centralized executive administration, judicial hierarchy, and civil services.",
    "detailedExplanation": "\n<h3>1. The Regulating Act of 1773</h3>\n<p>Enacted under Lord North following the Bengal Famine of 1770 and Company financial insolvency, it established the framework of modern Indian governance:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Governor-General of Bengal:</strong> Elevated the Governor of Bengal (Lord Warren Hastings) over Madras and Bombay Presidencies.</li>\n  <li><strong>Executive Council:</strong> Created a 4-member council with majority voting and a casting vote for the Governor-General.</li>\n  <li><strong>Supreme Court at Calcutta (1774):</strong> Established under Section 13 with Sir Elijah Impey as first Chief Justice, possessing Crown authority over British subjects.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>2. The Act of Settlement, 1781</h3>\n<p>Passed to curb judicial overreach by the Supreme Court into Company administration:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Exempted the Governor-General and Council from Supreme Court jurisdiction for acts performed in their official public capacity.</li>\n  <li>Excluded revenue collection from judicial interference.</li>\n  <li>Mandated application of personal laws of defendants (Hindu and Mohammedan personal laws).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>3. Pitt's India Act of 1784</h3>\n<p>Introduced the foundational \"Double Government\" mechanism:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Court of Directors:</strong> Controlled commercial affairs.</li>\n  <li><strong>Board of Control:</strong> 6 Crown commissioners managing civil, military, and revenue affairs.</li>\n  <li>Territories were formally designated the <em>\"British Possessions in India\"</em>.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>4. The Charter Acts of 1793, 1813, 1833, and 1853</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Charter Act 1793:</strong> Renewed monopoly for 20 years; charged Board of Control salaries to Indian revenues.</li>\n  <li><strong>Charter Act 1813:</strong> Terminated Company's Indian commercial monopoly except for tea and China trade; granted ₹1 lakh annual fund for education; permitted Christian missionaries.</li>\n  <li><strong>Charter Act 1833:</strong> Designated Governor-General of Bengal as the <strong>Governor-General of India</strong> (Lord William Bentinck); stripped Bombay and Madras of lawmaking powers; ended all commercial functions; introduced Macaulay as the 4th Law Member (First Law Commission, 1834).</li>\n  <li><strong>Charter Act 1853:</strong> Separated legislative from executive functions; created a 12-member Indian Legislative Council; introduced open competition for Indian Civil Services.</li>\n</ul>\n",
